    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s42003-026-10062-w",
    "abstract": "Phenological shifts enable species to adjust the timing of life-history events to required resources. Climate change alters the spatiotemporal association between temperature and photoperiod, modifying the scope for temperature regulation. Here, we propose a model hypothesizing that dissimilar light requirements and constraints differently impact phenological responses in diurnal versus nocturnal ectotherms. Next, we investigate temporal shifts and latitudinal trends in phenology for 363 Lepidoptera species using 44 years of citizen science data. In agreement with model predictions, long-term shifts in the estimated onset, peak, termination, and duration of the flight period differed qualitatively between diurnal and nocturnal species, even after accounting for voltinism and overwintering stage, supporting that diel activity is a key regulator of phenology. Phenology showed intraspecific latitudinal trends, and the peak occurred later in the north in diurnal species but was independent of latitude in nocturnal species. These contrasting phenological shifts may impact community composition and ecosystem functioning.",
    "field": "Phenology",
    "problem": "The influence of diel activity (diurnal vs. nocturnal behavior) on species' phenological responses to climate change has not been systematically investigated, despite differing light and temperature constraints that suggest these groups may respond differently.",
    "method": "The authors propose using quantile regression on 44 years of citizen science data for 363 Lepidoptera species to model and compare temporal shifts and latitudinal trends in flight period phenology between diurnal and nocturnal groups, while statistically accounting for voltinism and overwintering stage.",
    "task": "The task is to investigate and contrast temporal shifts and latitudinal trends in the phenology of diurnal versus nocturnal Lepidoptera under climate change, using 44 years of citizen science data to test a model based on how diel activity modulates phenological responses.",
